#	Handwritten Image Classifier & CalmDigit (Mood Visualizer)

Two Streamlit apps powered by a small CNN trained on MNIST:
	1.	Digit Classifier (app.py) – predicts the digit in an uploaded image or canvas drawing.
	2.	CalmDigit (mood.py) – you draw a number (0–9) as your energy level; the app interprets it into a mood card, gives a short reflection/breathing flow, and learns from your corrections.

Live demos:
	•	Digit Classifier: https://handwritten-image-classifier-gnvfmpuqqsabwve3e8fysf.streamlit.app/
	•	CalmDigit: https://handwritten-image-classifier-5ylaqfpgvmrrnrsc4viz77.streamlit.app/

These are non-medical, educational demos.

⸻

Repository Structure

.
├── app.py                 # Simple digit classifier app
├── mood.py                # CalmDigit app with reflection & feedback memory
├── mnist_cnn.pt           # Trained PyTorch weights (CNN for MNIST)
├── requirements.txt       # Dependencies to run locally
├── single_numbers.ipynb   # Notebook for experiments
└── README.md              # This file


⸻

Features

Shared
	•	PyTorch CNN (2 conv layers + FC) trained on MNIST
	•	Robust preprocessing (center, deskew, invert if needed)
	•	CPU/GPU/MPS device selection (CUDA on Nvidia, MPS on Apple Silicon)

CalmDigit extras (mood.py)
	•	Converts predicted digit → mood band (Low / Balanced / High) with short guidance
	•	“Calm & reflect” breathing micro-exercise (box-breathing)
	•	Lightweight on-device learning: if the prediction is wrong, click the correct digit; the app stores a small vector in feedback_mem.npz and uses it to adjust future predictions for similar drawings (cosine similarity).

⸻

Quickstart (Run Locally)

0) Prerequisites
	•	Python 3.10+ recommended
	•	macOS (Intel/Apple Silicon), Linux, or Windows
	•	(Optional) GPU: CUDA (Nvidia) or MPS on Apple Silicon (macOS 12.3+)

1) Clone and enter the folder

git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/handwritten-image-classifier.git
cd handwritten-image-classifier

2) Create and activate a virtual environment (recommended)

python3 -m venv .venv
# macOS/Linux:
source .venv/bin/activate
# Windows (PowerShell):
# .venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

3) Install dependencies

pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -r requirements.txt

If you hit build issues on Apple Silicon, try:

CFLAGS="-stdlib=libc++" pip install -r requirements.txt



4) Make sure the model file exists

mnist_cnn.pt must be in the same folder as app.py / mood.py.
(Already included in this repo.)

5) Run either app

Digit Classifier

streamlit run app.py

CalmDigit (Mood Visualizer)

streamlit run mood.py

Streamlit will print a local URL (e.g., http://localhost:8501). Open it in your browser.

⸻

Usage Notes

Digit Classifier (app.py)
	•	Upload a single grayscale or color image containing one digit, or draw on the canvas.
	•	The app preprocesses your image to 28×28 and predicts 0–9.

CalmDigit (mood.py)
	•	Draw a single number (0–9) representing your energy level today, then click Interpret.
	•	See the mood card + one-minute “Calm & reflect” box-breathing.
	•	If misread: click the correct number on the keypad → the app saves a small vector in feedback_mem.npz and will adjust future predictions for similar drawings.

To reset learning: delete feedback_mem.npz in the repo folder.

⸻

Troubleshooting
	•	Weights 'mnist_cnn.pt' not found
Ensure mnist_cnn.pt is in the repository root next to the app files.
	•	Port already in use
Run on a different port:

streamlit run mood.py --server.port 8502


	•	Apple Silicon performance
PyTorch will automatically use MPS if available. If you want CPU only:

FORCE_CPU=1 streamlit run mood.py


	•	Pip build issues
Upgrade pip/setuptools/wheel:

pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel



⸻

How the Model Works (Brief)

A compact CNN:
	•	Conv2d(1→10, k=5) → MaxPool → ReLU
	•	Conv2d(10→20, k=5) → Dropout2d → MaxPool → ReLU
	•	Flatten → Linear(320→50) → Dropout → Linear(50→10)
	•	Softmax for class probabilities

Training was done on MNIST; runtime preprocessing recenters, denoises, and normalizes input to improve real-world sketches.

⸻

Local Development Tips
	•	Streamlit auto-reloads when you save changes. If it doesn’t, press R in the terminal.
	•	Add new UI elements and experiments in a separate branch; keep requirements.txt minimal for Streamlit Cloud deploys.

